This study examines the following parabolic differential forms of the complex Monge-Ampere and H equations:
$$\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} &= f_{1}(\frac{w}{2}\omega_{\pi}^{\otimes m}) \\
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} &= f_{2}(\frac{w}{2}\omega_{F}^{\otimes m})
\end{aligned} \right.$$ where $\pi$ denotes a Hermitian-Einstein map from an algebraic vector bundle $E$ over a compact Kähler manifold $X$, and $F$ represents an associated curvature form. The operator acts as a linear combination involving the fundamental forms associated with these manifolds, normalized appropriately. The functionals $f_{1}, f_{1} \in C^{\infty}$ depend smoothly on certain symmetric functions associated with eigenvalues of the underlying tensors. Under appropriate assumptions on these functions and initial data, existence and uniqueness results are obtained for solutions to these PDE systems. Furthermore, the proof establishes that the obtained solution is uniformly bounded in time, with bounds independent of the parameter $\epsilon$. This allows one to extract a subsequence converging weakly to a continuous limit function $u(t)$, which satisfies the corresponding elliptic Monge-Ampere or H equations respectively. The main difficulty lies precisely in establishing this uniform
